Description: Learns a directed acyclic graph (DAG) from observational data
    that follow a linear structural equation model. Each error may be
    Gaussian or a Gaussian scale mixture. The method needs neither the error
    distributions nor the identities of the nodes with non-Gaussian errors.
    The method, called LEWIS (Laplace Error Working model for Inference and
    Selection of DAG), uses a Laplace working likelihood at every node, a
    complexity prior on the graph and a Laplace approximation of the
    marginal likelihood. Under mild conditions, the posterior probability of
    the distribution equivalence class of the true DAG tends to one as the
    sample size grows. DAGs are sampled with a Metropolis-Hastings
    algorithm. The draws are summarized by a point estimate of the
    distribution equivalence class, a credible ball and posterior
    probabilities of edges and of non-Gaussian errors. The point estimate is
    a restricted partially directed acyclic graph. The package includes gene
    expression data of the androgen-receptor program in prostate cancer from
    The Cancer Genome Atlas.
